    passageB1: `<p>Imagine you have to <strong>choose</strong> between saving a person or an animal. What would you do? Most adults say they would save the person. But a new study from Poland shows that many young children would rather save the animal.</p>

<p>Matti Wilks, a researcher from the UK, said that this <strong>finding</strong> was very surprising. Adults usually think that human life is more important than animal life. For example, a big <strong>survey</strong> asked many people what self-driving cars should do in an accident. Most people said the car should hit a dog or cat instead of a person.</p>

<p>But new <strong>evidence</strong> suggests that young children may think differently. Wilks and her team used a toy train and Lego figures to ask 170 children (aged 6 to 9) what they would do in a difficult situation. This situation is called the “trolley problem.”</p>

<p>The children had to decide if they would make the train go down a track and hit a Lego person or a Lego animal (a dog or a chimpanzee). The researchers also asked 178 adults (aged 18 to 50) the same question, to <strong>compare</strong> their answers.</p>

<p>About 42% of the children said they would save the dog and let the train hit the person. Only 17% of the adults said the same thing. Also, about 28% of the children said they would save the chimpanzee over the person. Only 11% of the adults agreed with them.</p>

<p>Wilks and her team had also done a similar study in the US. They found that 28% of children (aged 5 to 9) said they would save a dog instead of a person from a sinking ship. Only 8% of adults said the same. In that study, 18% of children said they would save a pig over a person, <strong>compared to</strong> 3% of adults.</p>

<p>Karri Neldner, a researcher from Germany, says that children <strong>learn</strong> from their parents and teachers to care about others. It might be easier for them to think that this rule <strong>applies to</strong> both humans and animals. So, they don't have a strong preference for saving one over the other.</p>

<p>Neldner also says that as children get older, they might start to <strong>understand</strong> that it's more important to care for other people. Her research shows that this change starts around the age of 10. She asked children (aged 4 to 10) to put pictures of people, animals, plants, and objects in order of how much they cared about them.</p>

<p>The 4-year-olds cared more about dogs, cats, and dolphins than about their classmates, police officers, and sick people. But the 10-year-olds cared more about the people. The reason children <strong>value</strong> dogs so much might be because they are more familiar with them, says Wilks. Her studies show that children who spend more time with dogs are more likely to say they would save a dog over a person. </p>

<p>In <strong>conclusion</strong>, this research shows that young children often <strong>prioritize</strong> animal lives over human lives, which is different from most adults. This may be because children learn to care for all living things, and it is only later that they learn to value human life more.</p>`,
    translationB1: `<p>Tasavvur qiling, siz odamni qutqarish yoki hayvonni qutqarish o'rtasida <strong>tanlov</strong> qilishga majbursiz. Nima qilardingiz? Ko'pchilik kattalar odamni qutqarishini aytadi. Ammo Polshadan olingan yangi tadqiqot shuni ko'rsatadiki, ko'plab yosh bolalar hayvonni qutqarishni afzal ko'rishadi.</p>

<p>Buyuk Britaniyalik tadqiqotchi Matti Wilksning aytishicha, bu <strong>natija</strong> juda hayratlanarli. Kattalar odatda inson hayoti hayvon hayotidan muhimroq deb hisoblashadi. Masalan, katta <strong>so'rov</strong> ko'plab odamlardan o'z-o'zidan harakatlanuvchi mashinalar baxtsiz hodisada nima qilishi kerakligini so'radi. Ko'pchilik mashina odam o'rniga it yoki mushukni urishi kerakligini aytdi.</p>

<p>Ammo yangi <strong>dalillar</strong> shuni ko'rsatadiki, yosh bolalar boshqacha fikrlashi mumkin. Wilks va uning jamoasi 170 nafar boladan (6 yoshdan 9 yoshgacha) qiyin vaziyatda nima qilishlarini so'rash uchun o'yinchoq poyezd va Lego figuralaridan foydalanishdi. Bu vaziyat "trolley muammosi" deb ataladi.</p>

<p>Bolalar poyezdni relsdan pastga tushirib, Lego odami yoki Lego hayvonini (it yoki shimpanze) urishga qaror qilishlari kerak edi. Tadqiqotchilar, shuningdek, 178 nafar kattalarga (18 yoshdan 50 yoshgacha) ularning javoblarini <strong>taqqoslash</strong> uchun xuddi shu savolni berishdi.</p>

<p>Bolalarning taxminan 42 foizi itni qutqarishini va poyezd odamni urishiga yo'l qo'yishini aytdi. Kattalarning atigi 17 foizi xuddi shunday dedi. Shuningdek, bolalarning taxminan 28 foizi odamdan ko'ra shimpanzeni qutqarishini aytdi. Kattalarning atigi 11 foizi ularga qo'shildi.</p>

<p>Wilks va uning jamoasi AQShda ham shunga o'xshash tadqiqot o'tkazishdi. Ular 5 yoshdan 9 yoshgacha bo'lgan bolalarning 28 foizi cho'kayotgan kemadan odam o'rniga itni qutqarishini aytishgan. Kattalarning atigi 8 foizi xuddi shunday dedi. Ushbu tadqiqotda bolalarning 18 foizi odamdan ko'ra cho'chqani qutqarishini aytishdi, <strong>taqqoslaganda</strong> kattalarning 3 foizi.</p>

<p>Germaniyalik tadqiqotchi Karri Neldnerning aytishicha, bolalar ota-onalari va o'qituvchilaridan boshqalarga g'amxo'rlik qilishni <strong>o'rganadilar</strong>. Ularga bu qoida ham odamlarga, ham hayvonlarga <strong>tegishli ekanligini</strong> o'ylash osonroq bo'lishi mumkin. Shunday qilib, ularning birini ikkinchisidan ko'ra qutqarishga kuchli afzalligi yo'q.</p>

<p>Neldnerning ta'kidlashicha, bolalar ulg'aygan sari, boshqa odamlarga g'amxo'rlik qilish muhimroq ekanligini <strong>tushunishlari</strong> mumkin. Uning tadqiqotlari shuni ko'rsatadiki, bu o'zgarish taxminan 10 yoshdan boshlanadi. U bolalardan (4 yoshdan 10 yoshgacha) odamlarning, hayvonlarning, o'simliklarning va narsalarning rasmlarini ularga qanchalik g'amxo'rlik qilish tartibida joylashtirishni so'radi.</p>

<p>4 yoshli bolalar sinfdoshlari, politsiyachilar va kasal odamlarga qaraganda itlar, mushuklar va delfinlarga ko'proq g'amxo'rlik qilishdi. Ammo 10 yoshli bolalar odamlarga ko'proq g'amxo'rlik qilishdi. Bolalarning itlarni bunchalik <strong>qadrlashi</strong> sababi ularning ular bilan yaxshiroq tanishligi bo'lishi mumkin, deydi Wilks. Uning tadqiqotlari shuni ko'rsatadiki, itlar bilan ko'proq vaqt o'tkazadigan bolalar odamdan ko'ra itni qutqarishini aytish ehtimoli ko'proq.</p>

<p><strong>Xulosa qilib aytganda</strong>, bu tadqiqot shuni ko'rsatadiki, yosh bolalar ko'pincha hayvonlar hayotini inson hayotidan ustun <strong>qo'yishadi</strong>, bu ko'pchilik kattalardan farq qiladi. Buning sababi shundaki, bolalar barcha tirik mavjudotlarga g'amxo'rlik qilishni o'rganadilar va faqat keyinroq inson hayotini qadrlashni o'rganadilar.</p>`,
